13. OFFLINE-FIRST LEARNING AND CODING

Offline functionality is a core requirement.

The platform must clearly distinguish local functionality from services that require an internet connection.

13.1 Offline Features

Support offline access to:

- Previously cached application pages.
- Downloaded learning resources.
- Previously downloaded notes.
- Saved code projects.
- Local project editing.
- Supported local code execution.
- Saved bookmarks.
- Local learning progress.
- Queued actions that can safely synchronize later.

13.2 Offline Storage

Use:

- IndexedDB for browser project data and structured offline records.
- Service Workers for application asset caching.
- Platform-appropriate local storage on desktop and mobile.

Avoid relying on localStorage for large files or complex project storage.

13.3 Offline Execution

Support local execution only when the required runtime or compiler is installed and available.

Examples:

- Browser-based HTML/CSS/JavaScript.
- Python when a compatible local runtime is available.
- C/C++ when a local compiler is installed.
- Dart when a compatible local SDK is installed.
- Node.js when a local runtime is installed.

Other languages and frameworks require their respective runtimes, compilers, SDKs, and dependencies.

13.4 Synchronization

When connectivity returns:

- Detect the connection.
- Synchronize queued changes.
- Retry recoverable failures.
- Avoid duplicate submissions.
- Resolve conflicting project updates.
- Display synchronization status.
- Preserve local work when synchronization fails.

Use unique operation identifiers or idempotency mechanisms where appropriate.

13.5 Offline Limitations

Clearly explain when these features require connectivity:

- Live video and audio.
- Real-time messaging.
- Internet research.
- Cloud compilation.
- Remote AI services.
- Cloud-hosted databases.
- Remote project collaboration.

Provide a clear online/offline indicator.

